1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,765
Everything you are watching right now, the slides, the animations, the voice, and the captions, was made by two agent skills.

2
00:00:08,765 --> 00:00:11,875
No video editor, no microphone, and no retakes.

3
00:00:13,967 --> 00:00:15,327
It takes two skills.

4
00:00:15,327 --> 00:00:19,407
First, the reveal J S skill builds a presentation you can present.

5
00:00:19,407 --> 00:00:23,147
Then the vidcast skill turns that deck into a narrated video.

6
00:00:23,147 --> 00:00:28,587
You don't need to know how reveal J S, text to speech, or the syncing works.

7
00:00:28,587 --> 00:00:30,627
Your agent handles all of it.

8
00:00:30,627 --> 00:00:34,367
If you are curious what happens inside the skills, keep watching.

9
00:00:36,467 --> 00:00:38,054
So, what happens inside?

10
00:00:38,054 --> 00:00:43,612
Turning slides into a video usually means recording yourself, and that brings three problems.

11
00:00:43,612 --> 00:00:46,390
Retakes: one stumble, and you start again.

12
00:00:46,390 --> 00:00:51,550
Drift: the screen recorder lags, so animations land a beat after the words.

13
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:55,917
Edits: change one slide, and you record the whole thing again.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,855
The pipeline has five stages.

15
00:00:59,855 --> 00:01:02,080
First, build a deck with animations.

16
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:04,676
Next, write a script with cue markers.

17
00:01:04,676 --> 00:01:06,902
Then synthesise a voice with Kokoro.

18
00:01:06,902 --> 00:01:09,127
Turn the spoken words into timing.

19
00:01:09,127 --> 00:01:12,095
And finally, render the video, frame by frame.

20
00:01:12,095 --> 00:01:18,400
Two skills split the work: one builds the deck, and the other turns it into the video.

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,024
Stage one is the deck, built with the reveal J S skill.

22
00:01:25,024 --> 00:01:28,794
It starts from the story, giving every slide one job.

23
00:01:28,794 --> 00:01:32,564
Then it chooses a composition, preferring real evidence over decoration.

24
00:01:32,564 --> 00:01:39,350
Animations are fragments: each one is a step the narrator will reach, in the order it is spoken.

25
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:43,272
Stage two is the script.

26
00:01:43,272 --> 00:01:50,496
The narration lives in each slide's speaker notes, with a cue marker right before the word that reveals each step.

27
00:01:50,496 --> 00:01:56,997
The rule is simple: one marker per step, placed before the noun, not the start of the sentence.

28
00:01:56,997 --> 00:02:01,692
Before rendering, an inspect script compares steps to markers, and flags any mismatch.

29
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:05,651
Stage three is the voice.

30
00:02:05,651 --> 00:02:11,574
Kokoro is an open-weight text to speech model on Hugging Face, with just eighty-two million parameters.

31
00:02:11,574 --> 00:02:17,867
It runs locally on a Mac, so there is no API key, and nothing leaves the machine.

32
00:02:17,867 --> 00:02:23,050
And it returns word timestamps: a first estimate of when each word is spoken.

33
00:02:25,133 --> 00:02:26,531
Stage four is timing.

34
00:02:26,531 --> 00:02:31,774
Kokoro's estimate can be half a second off, especially at the start of a sentence.

35
00:02:31,774 --> 00:02:37,366
So Whisper transcribes each clip, and the cue becomes the moment the word is actually heard.

36
00:02:37,366 --> 00:02:42,958
The step then fires a tenth of a second early, which reads as perfectly in sync.

37
00:02:45,067 --> 00:02:46,906
Stage five renders the video.

38
00:02:46,906 --> 00:02:52,424
A screen recorder runs in real time, and under load it can fall seconds behind.

39
00:02:52,424 --> 00:03:00,517
Instead, the renderer uses a virtual clock: timers and CSS animations are paused, then seeked to the exact time of every frame.

40
00:03:00,517 --> 00:03:08,242
So a step fired at frame N is on screen at frame N, no matter how slowly the frames are captured.

41
00:03:10,333 --> 00:03:12,691
Each render produces more than a video.

42
00:03:12,691 --> 00:03:15,722
The M P 4, with the narration muxed in.

43
00:03:15,722 --> 00:03:19,763
An S R T caption track, timed from the same narration clips.

44
00:03:19,763 --> 00:03:24,814
A timeline file, recording when each cue word is spoken and when its step fires.

45
00:03:24,814 --> 00:03:33,233
And a sync check, which listens to the finished video, watches the screen, and fails any step that lags by more than half a second.

46
00:03:35,333 --> 00:03:38,886
And you never have to run any of this by hand.

47
00:03:38,886 --> 00:03:40,823
Every script ships inside the skill.

48
00:03:40,823 --> 00:03:45,668
The skill file tells your agent how to run them, and how to fix them.

49
00:03:45,668 --> 00:03:48,897
The scripts handle setup, inspection, rendering, and the sync check.

50
00:03:48,897 --> 00:03:55,033
And if your Python environment is different, your agent is smart enough to adapt the setup to match it.

51
00:03:57,133 --> 00:03:59,513
One thing does depend on your model.

52
00:03:59,513 --> 00:04:04,952
The design and story are written by the model: the layout, the animations, and the script.

53
00:04:04,952 --> 00:04:06,651
Stronger models make better decks.

54
00:04:06,651 --> 00:04:13,790
The voice, timing, and render are done by scripts, so whichever model you use, you still get a working, synced video.

55
00:04:13,790 --> 00:04:16,509
For the best design, use a strong model.

56
00:04:16,509 --> 00:04:19,908
This deck was designed by Claude Opus five point five.

57
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,208
It is also cheap to run.

58
00:04:24,208 --> 00:04:30,833
The model writes one HTML file, and everything after that is code, so very few tokens are spent.

59
00:04:30,833 --> 00:04:34,882
You need no image or video model, only a text model.

60
00:04:34,882 --> 00:04:41,875
A multimodal model does help: the agent takes Playwright screenshots of each slide, and checks that everything renders correctly.

61
00:04:43,967 --> 00:04:44,973
So, two skills.

62
00:04:44,973 --> 00:04:47,656
The reveal J S skill builds the deck.

63
00:04:47,656 --> 00:04:50,675
The vidcast skill turns it into a narrated video.

64
00:04:50,675 --> 00:04:53,023
Links to both are in the post.

65
00:04:53,023 --> 00:04:56,042
Make a deck, and let it speak for itself.

